+## The `nopodman` profile — doors, no walls
+
+`profiles/nopodman.ts` (proven by `features/nopodman.feature` and
+`nopodman.test.ts`, which run here without any container runtime) opens a room
+on a host that has no `podman` at all: the guest shares a filesystem and a uid
+with its brokers. It does the part of a launch that survives losing the walls,
+and refuses the parts that would lie:
+
+- **Same-host doors.** Every door's guest side *is* its host side — a child
+  connects at the path the broker listens on. Paths go to `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`,
+  else `$HOME/.guest-room/run` (0700), never `/tmp`; a world-writable socket dir
+  is refused. `childEnv` is exactly the `<NAME>_SOCK` map to hand a child under
+  `env -i`.
+- **Injecting parent refused unless acknowledged.** A set `HTTPS_PROXY` is the
+  only signal from inside, so it is treated as injecting (fail closed);
+  `GUEST_ROOM_ALLOW_INJECTING_PARENT=1` opts in, and the rulebook then names the
+  gateway and says a door cannot deny reach or strip identity.
+- **An honest rulebook.** The preamble says *NO WALLS* in as many words; the
+  denied section still names every absent door.
+- **An allowlist door** (`profiles/allowlist-door.ts`) — the egress door's
+  *decision* without its *chokepoint*: `connect {host}` is answered from the
+  grant's `host=` caveats via `checkCaveats` (fail closed) over a real unix
+  socket, every verdict logged, and a door with no `host=` caveat refuses to
+  serve at all. It is a decision oracle a child asks, not a proxy the bytes
+  pass through — see G2.
+
+```ts
+import { nopodmanRoom } from "@bounded-systems/guest-room/profiles/nopodman";
+const room = nopodmanRoom(rooms, catalog, "survey", process.env,
+  { caveats: { net: ["host=github.com"] } });
+// room.grants  — same-host DoorGrants;  room.childEnv — { NET_SOCK: "…/net.sock" }
+// room.rulebook — the preamble says NO WALLS; DENIED still names every absent door
+```
+
+### Gap list — what a Cowork sandbox cannot do, and why
+
+Each gap is named so the rulebook, the profile and this page agree on what is
+*not* enforced. "Here" means a default Cowork sandbox as measured above (uid 0,
+no `podman`, no user namespaces, egress only via the injecting gateway).
+
+| Gap | What is missing | Why it is not implemented here |
+|---|---|---|
+| **G1 — no walls** | `--network=none`, `--cap-drop`, rootless uid mapping, a mounted-only door view | Needs a container runtime or unprivileged user namespaces; neither exists here and a VM that is uid 0 cannot self-confine. Only `env -i` + `setpriv` remain — a seatbelt a careless launch skips, not a cage. Documented, not faked: the profile's rulebook says *NO WALLS*. |
+| **G2 — no egress chokepoint** | The consumer's egress daemon as a forward proxy the bytes pass through (`CONNECT` + allowlist, fail closed) | The reference runner needs `podman` + `docker.io/ubuntu/squid` + `socat`; the bun-native daemon needs no container but lives in the consumer repo and, here, would itself have to chain every `CONNECT` through the injecting gateway — which already decides reach and adds identity, so a second proxy cannot deny what the first allows. The profile ships the *decision* (`allowlist-door.ts`) and states it is not a proxy. |
+| **G3 — reach and identity are platform dials** | A door-level allowlist that means *may reach* | The gateway injects credentials past any door (measured above). Reach is `Network access → Allowed domains`; identity is the repo attach set. A door here can only narrow *intent*, so the profile names the gateway in the rulebook rather than claiming to police it. |
+| **G4 — injecting-parent rule not in the consumer's daemon** | The egress daemon refusing to serve behind `HTTPS_PROXY` unless told otherwise | The rule exists only in this profile (`GUEST_ROOM_ALLOW_INJECTING_PARENT`). The consumer vendors this repo as a subdirectory, so nothing landed here reaches it without a separate sync PR — cross-repo work this session cannot claim. |
+| **G5 — no `work.sh` launcher** | `./work.sh survey\|land <cmd>`: `env -i` + `setpriv` + the profile's `childEnv` around a command | The rooms are the consumer's catalog, not this repo's; a launcher that names `survey`/`land` belongs there (and would carry the G1 caveat verbatim). The profile is the piece a launcher calls. |
+| **G6 — peer credentials are uninformative** | `SO_PEERCRED` distinguishing the guest from the brokers | Everything in the VM runs as uid 0, so the kernel vouches for the same identity on both ends. A unix door here is gated by the private run dir only; `tokenAuthorizer` / `hmacAuthorizer` would add nothing a same-uid process cannot read. |
+| **G7 — bun is root-only** | An unprivileged child running a TypeScript broker or client | `bun` lives under `/root` (mode 0700) and `CURL_CA_BUNDLE` points into `/root/.ccr`. After `setpriv` a child can call a door only with stdlib tooling (e.g. Python) speaking the newline-JSON protocol; the brokers must run as root. Re-implementing the client in Python is out of scope for this repo. |
+| **G8 — no signed grants in transit** | The concierge (`keys`) a tcp/vsock egress door verifies grants against | Unix-only here (G6 makes tcp add nothing), and the concierge is the consumer's daemon. `signedGrantAuthorizer` is available if a tcp door is ever wanted across sandboxes. |
+
+Every row above is a statement about the substrate, not a defect in the engine:
+the engine's algebra (`bun test`) is green here without `podman`.
+
 ## Known traps
 
 - **The CA bundle is wired by env var into a root-only path.** Children inherit
